MASTER EXECUTIVE DIRECTIVE

Build LINGUAVERSE AI as a premium, intelligent, voice-first language-learning and real-world communication training platform.

This is not another vocabulary memorization application, grammar quiz platform, or generic AI chatbot.

LINGUAVERSE AI must connect five activities:

Learning a language.

Practicing communication.

Detecting actual mistakes.

Correcting weaknesses.

Measuring whether the learner improves.

The platform must create a continuous, evidence-based learning experience.

0.1 Non-negotiable principles

Never claim functionality that has not been implemented and tested.

Never generate fake user activity, learning progress, revenue, reviews, or analytics.

Never present AI-generated guesses as verified educational assessments.

Never award proficiency levels without sufficient assessment evidence.

Never claim pronunciation accuracy without appropriate acoustic evidence.

Never expose API keys, credentials, or provider secrets in frontend code.

Never bypass backend authorization.

Never use private voice recordings for model training without appropriate consent and legal basis.

Never overwrite an existing repository without auditing it.

Never create duplicate components, tables, services, or API routes unnecessarily.

Never delete production data through routine migrations.

Every important operation must have a verifiable backend implementation.

Every AI evaluation must be testable, explainable, and traceable.

Every sensitive administrative operation must be audited.

Every implementation phase must produce evidence-based completion reports.

Accessibility, privacy, reliability, and performance are product requirements, not optional polish.

0.2 Product success philosophy

The objective is not to maximize:

Time spent unnecessarily.

Number of AI conversations.

Vocabulary cards opened.

Daily meaningless page visits.

Artificial engagement.

The objective is to improve:

Communication ability.

Language comprehension.

Sentence construction.

Speaking performance.

Knowledge retention.

Real-world task completion.

Learner confidence as self-reported and behaviorally observed.

